#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064059} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67f41769-a0ff-5ecc-8b05-5890a7ac9fe3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsecant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064049} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9548e6-28ef-5a97-ab04-dfb1b6e23c16" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064050} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bd8c6d8-0b11-5735-88a1-9b12e6315401" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064051} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064182 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064056} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90033e91-fdb2-5aa0-8cce-76762a38ffd2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsine"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064057} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7256d2e-7105-5a4e-8d0a-5e00dfc1e311" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arctangent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064058} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e6b5d85-0b53-580f-9520-6c33463231d5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions and Some Manipulations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065407 :tutorial/content "Remember that the exponential function $e^x$ has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right)  = e^ x.\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d2a6d036-0881-5a58-8935-905cd004a7e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065408 :tutorial/content "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left( a^ x\\right)\t=\ta^ x \\ln a.\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int  a^x \\, \\textrm d x  = \\dfrac{a^ x }{\\ln a} +C.\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x   &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "254d6a12-01a6-5a3b-b0fd-fbefc017a149" :tutorial/math-academy-id 714 :tutorial/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065410 :tutorial/content "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse trigonometric functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}x) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arcsec}\\,x) & =\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, we arrive at the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}(x)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =\\text{arcsec}(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a238ada9-9756-56cd-992d-a26ce54ab913" :tutorial/math-academy-id 723 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068447} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068448} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}]}
